course: dictionary
lesson: 3367
title: Exclusive: only for some
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Exclusive means only for one person or group, not shared with others. «Countries also have borders at sea, with territorial waters reaching 12 nautical miles from the coast, and fishing and mineral rights in an exclusive economic zone up to 200 nautical miles out.» The clue is fishing and mineral rights: in that zone, only one country gets to fish and mine there.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. "A zone only for one country" fits, so exclusive means only for one group. An exclusive club or shop works the same way: it lets in only certain people, so it is often expensive.
explain: Be careful, because exclusive has other uses. «The price is £10, exclusive of tax» uses exclusive of, which means not including. So tax is not in the £10, and you must still add it.
fact: Exclusive means only for one person or group, not shared with others.
fact: «An exclusive shop» or «an exclusive club» is expensive and lets in only certain people.
fact: «An exclusive» (noun) is a news story that only one newspaper or channel has.
fact: Exclusive of means not including, as in «the price is exclusive of tax», so the tax must still be added.
fact: Mutually exclusive events cannot both happen at the same time, like a coin landing on heads and tails at once.
exercise: «The exclusive economic zone gives one country the right to fish up to 200 nautical miles out.» What does exclusive mean here? | *Only for one group, not shared with others | Very expensive to enter | Open to every boat in the world | Stormy and dangerous
exercise: The price is £20, exclusive of tax. What does exclusive of mean? | Including tax | Cheaper than before | *Not including, so tax must still be added | Free of charge
exercise: Which club lets in only certain people? | A public park | A library open to everyone | A school playground | *An exclusive club
test: What are events called when they cannot both happen at once? | Ancient | *Mutually exclusive | Shared | Glum
test: A newspaper prints an exclusive story. What does that mean? | A story about old castles | A short, sad poem | Read by everyone for free | *A news story that only one newspaper or channel has
exam: Which sentence uses exclusive to mean not including? | An exclusive interview with the star | *The price is £10, exclusive of tax | Only members may enter the exclusive club | Ask for an exclusive deal
